    - id: conventions
      markdown: "# Engineering conventions\n\nGive contributors a shared vocabulary.\n\n## Naming and nomenclature\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.1 -->\ncamelCase variables, PascalCase components, kebab-case files; “request” (not ticket or case) everywhere; identifiers REQ-, UC-, ADR-, CMP-, RISK-, TEST-.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Repository and folder structure\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.2 -->\napps/web, apps/api, packages/shared; unit tests beside code as *.test.ts; end-to-end tests in tests/e2e; specification in .plan/.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Backend and frontend boundaries\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.3 -->\nAuthorization and persistence only in apps/api; zod schemas in packages/shared; the web app never imports from apps/api.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Lint and formatting rules\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.4 -->\nPrettier (100 columns, single quotes) and ESLint with typescript-eslint; CI fails on any warning.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Error handling conventions\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.5 -->\nThrow HttpError with a status and a safe message; unexpected errors are logged server-side and return a generic message.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Documentation conventions\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.6 -->\nEvery pull request updates .plan/tasks.md and changes.md; architectural changes add an ADR.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Do and do not examples\n\n<!-- sdd:answer conventions.7 -->\n| Do | Don’t | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Validate with the shared schema | Hand-written checks in routes | One source of truth for contracts |\n| Filter by organizationId in the data layer | Filter in the UI only | Prevents cross-tenant leaks |\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n"
      diagram: "flowchart TD\n  root[repository] --> apps[apps]\n  root --> packages[packages]\n  root --> docs[docs]\n  apps --> web[web: user interface]\n  apps --> api[api: HTTP service]\n  packages --> shared[shared: schemas and domain]\n  web -.->|imports| shared\n  api -.->|imports| shared"
      answers:
        conventions.1:
          value: "camelCase variables, PascalCase components, kebab-case files; “request” (not ticket or case) everywhere; identifiers REQ-, UC-, ADR-, CMP-, RISK-, TEST-."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        conventions.2:
          value: "apps/web, apps/api, packages/shared; unit tests beside code as *.test.ts; end-to-end tests in tests/e2e; specification in .plan/."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        conventions.3:
          value: Authorization and persistence only in apps/api; zod schemas in packages/shared; the web app never imports from apps/api.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        conventions.4:
          value: "Prettier (100 columns, single quotes) and ESLint with typescript-eslint; CI fails on any warning."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        conventions.5:
          value: Throw HttpError with a status and a safe message; unexpected errors are logged server-side and return a generic message.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        conventions.6:
          value: Every pull request updates .plan/tasks.md and changes.md; architectural changes add an ADR.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        conventions.7:
          value: "[[\"Validate with the shared schema\",\"Hand-written checks in routes\",\"One source of truth for contracts\"],[\"Filter by organizationId in the data layer\",\"Filter in the UI only\",\"Prevents cross-tenant leaks\"]]"
          state: answered
          reason: ""
      status: draft
      references[2]{id,title,url,kind}:
        conventions-ref-1,Conventional Commits,"https://www.conventionalcommits.org/",official
        conventions-ref-2,Semantic Versioning,"https://semver.org/",official
    - id: testing
      markdown: "# Testing & quality\n\nConnect every important promise to a test.\n\n## Test pyramid and ownership\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.1 -->\nUnit tests for domain rules (developers, < 30 s); API integration tests against PostgreSQL (< 3 min); 5 end-to-end journeys (QA lead).\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Unit test boundaries\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.2 -->\nState transitions, permission checks and validation; no mocks of our own domain code; 80% branch coverage for packages/shared.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Integration and contract tests\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.3 -->\nAPI tests run against PostgreSQL in Docker (Testcontainers); responses validated against the OpenAPI document; email provider replaced by a contract stub.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## End-to-end acceptance journeys\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.4 -->\nSign in → submit request → agent replies → customer sees reply (REQ-001, REQ-003, REQ-004); cross-organization access denied (REQ-002).\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Performance and security tests\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.5 -->\nk6 load test at 2× expected peak before release; dependency and secret scanning in CI; external penetration test before general availability.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Accessibility tests\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.6 -->\naxe checks in every end-to-end test (no serious violations); manual NVDA pass on the three main journeys each release.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Fixtures and test data\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.7 -->\nA seed script creates 3 organizations and 50 requests with synthetic names; each run uses a fresh database.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Quality gates and release criteria\n\n<!-- sdd:answer testing.8 -->\nMerge: lint, typecheck, unit and integration tests, no high-severity vulnerabilities. Release: end-to-end suite green and product owner sign-off.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n"
      diagram: "flowchart TB\n  e2e[End-to-end: few critical journeys]\n  integration[Integration and contract: real dependencies]\n  unit[Unit: domain rules, many and fast]\n  e2e --- integration\n  integration --- unit"
      answers:
        testing.1:
          value: "Unit tests for domain rules (developers, < 30 s); API integration tests against PostgreSQL (< 3 min); 5 end-to-end journeys (QA lead)."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.2:
          value: "State transitions, permission checks and validation; no mocks of our own domain code; 80% branch coverage for packages/shared."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.3:
          value: API tests run against PostgreSQL in Docker (Testcontainers); responses validated against the OpenAPI document; email provider replaced by a contract stub.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.4:
          value: "Sign in → submit request → agent replies → customer sees reply (REQ-001, REQ-003, REQ-004); cross-organization access denied (REQ-002)."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.5:
          value: k6 load test at 2× expected peak before release; dependency and secret scanning in CI; external penetration test before general availability.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.6:
          value: axe checks in every end-to-end test (no serious violations); manual NVDA pass on the three main journeys each release.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.7:
          value: A seed script creates 3 organizations and 50 requests with synthetic names; each run uses a fresh database.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        testing.8:
          value: "Merge: lint, typecheck, unit and integration tests, no high-severity vulnerabilities. Release: end-to-end suite green and product owner sign-off."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
      status: draft
      references[2]{id,title,url,kind}:
        testing-ref-1,The Practical Test Pyramid,"https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html",official
        testing-ref-2,OWASP Web Security Testing Guide,"https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/",official
    - id: delivery
      markdown: "# Delivery & environments\n\nMake delivery repeatable.\n\n## Development test and production environments\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.1 -->\nLocal (Docker Compose, seed data); staging (production-like, synthetic data, team access); production (customer data, on-call access only).\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Environment configuration and secrets\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.2 -->\n.env.example lists every variable; secrets come from the vault; the API refuses to start without required settings.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## CI/CD pipeline\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.3 -->\nGitHub Actions: lint → tests → scans → image build → staging deploy → manual approval → rolling production deploy.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Infrastructure and ownership\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.4 -->\nKubernetes manifests in the platform-infra repository, reviewed by Platform Engineering.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Docker installation\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.5 -->\n`docker compose up -d` starts the app, worker and PostgreSQL; data persists in a named volume; the app waits for a healthy database.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Non-Docker platform prerequisites\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.6 -->\nNode.js 24 and pnpm on all platforms; PostgreSQL 17 via winget (Windows), Homebrew (macOS) or the official apt repository (Linux).\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Migration and rollback plan\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.7 -->\nBack up before migration; deploy to 10% then 100%; roll back to the previous image tag if the error rate exceeds 2% for 5 minutes.\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Deployment targets\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.9 -->\n- Kubernetes\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Cloud and virtualization providers\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.10 -->\n- Microsoft Azure\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## CI/CD platform\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.11 -->\nGitHub Actions\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Pipeline stages\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.12 -->\n- Lint and format check\n- Type check / compile\n- Unit tests\n- Integration tests\n- End-to-end tests\n- Dependency scan\n- Secret scan\n- Build artifact / image\n- Deploy to staging\n- Manual approval\n- Deploy to production\n- Smoke tests\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Infrastructure as code\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.13 -->\nYes\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Server operating systems\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.14 -->\n- Ubuntu LTS\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Infrastructure-as-code tools\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.15 -->\n- Terraform\n- Helm\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## State and locking\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.16 -->\nRemote backend with locking (object storage)\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Infrastructure change workflow\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.17 -->\n- Plan shown on every pull request\n- Approval required before apply\n- Apply only from the pipeline\n- Short-lived cloud credentials (OIDC federation)\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Drift detection\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.18 -->\nYes\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n\n## Policy as code and IaC scanning\n\n<!-- sdd:answer delivery.19 -->\n- Checkov\n- Trivy (configuration scan)\n<!-- sdd:end -->\n"
      diagram: "flowchart LR\n  commit[Commit] --> checks[Lint and typecheck]\n  checks --> tests[Tests]\n  tests --> scan[Dependency and secret scan]\n  scan --> build[Build image]\n  build --> staging[Deploy to staging]\n  staging --> approve{Approval}\n  approve --> prod[Production]"
      answers:
        delivery.1:
          value: "Local (Docker Compose, seed data); staging (production-like, synthetic data, team access); production (customer data, on-call access only)."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        delivery.2:
          value: .env.example lists every variable; secrets come from the vault; the API refuses to start without required settings.
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        delivery.3:
          value: "GitHub Actions: lint → tests → scans → image build → staging deploy → manual approval → rolling production deploy."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        delivery.4:
          value: "Kubernetes manifests in the platform-infra repository, reviewed by Platform Engineering."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        delivery.5:
          value: "`docker compose up -d` starts the app, worker and PostgreSQL; data persists in a named volume; the app waits for a healthy database."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
        delivery.6:
          value: "Node.js 24 and pnpm on all platforms; PostgreSQL 17 via winget (Windows), Homebrew (macOS) or the official apt repository (Linux)."
          state: answered
          reason: ""
